      "math_academy_id": "q-61272",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(0,-2)$  and $(0,-4)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214315/assets/9edd6fb0f5b8dbf861a10cb559e597abaee54d4f833735912ab8ecb8b6e6091a.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $2$ units, so the distance equals $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214315/assets/ee815ded554b01fd73cfd3577cfd7a21ea1360a32414de62a2f43f8b8612fd67.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214315/assets/19d94e1c26e61bfccdbab7083781ea5fb164910c54719135b348a60002c27a0b.png)\n\nThe point $(0,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(0,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-2=2.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
